WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.150
Welcome the founder of The Honest Company, Jessica Alba!

2
00:00:05.326 --> 00:00:07.330
(audience applauding)

3
00:00:07.330 --> 00:00:09.055
Hi.
(applauding continues)

4
00:00:09.055 --> 00:00:10.140
Are we allowed to?

5
00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:11.291
We're not allowed to,

6
00:00:11.291 --> 00:00:12.417
isn't that horrible?
Oh no.

7
00:00:12.417 --> 00:00:14.470
I know, I love you so much.

8
00:00:14.470 --> 00:00:16.177
I'm so... (inhales)

9
00:00:16.177 --> 00:00:18.580
(exhales) I'm so happy for you.

10
00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:20.010
Thank you.

11
00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:24.410
My friend, this extraordinary woman who changed the world,

12
00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:28.490 line:15%
you IPO'd for Honest Company yesterday.

13
00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:30.950 line:15%
I saw you ringing the bell

14
00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:34.160 line:15%
with your beautiful child in your arms.

15
00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:39.160
Your company is going to be estimated at about $1.4 billion.

16
00:00:40.988 --> 00:00:45.601
(audience applauds)
(Jessica chuckles)

17
00:00:45.601 --> 00:00:47.350
I mean, when you were little,

18
00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:50.050
did you ever see this happening?

19
00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:52.170
No, I mean, this is like,

20
00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:54.680
I had this idea to create this company

21
00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:56.830
because it just made sense

22
00:00:56.830 --> 00:00:58.410
that people shouldn't have to choose

23
00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:01.450
between what works and what's good for them.

24
00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:04.410
And putting people's health and wellness,

25
00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:07.710 line:15%
and thinking about the planet and sustainability,

26
00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:10.450 line:15%
and then also like diversity and inclusion

27
00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:14.070 line:15%
and representation, and all of those values,

28
00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:17.210 line:15%
to me is really I think,

29
00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:22.025
why we've been able to take the business to this point.

30
00:01:22.025 --> 00:01:25.210
And also the connection with charity and Baby2Baby

31
00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:27.670
and giving back, which you're also part of.

32
00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:32.670
So, I feel like this is the beginning of our next chapter.

33
00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:39.350
With my kids and my husband, it's been such a journey

34
00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:41.610
getting to this point and I'm just thrilled.

35
00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:45.950
And I hope that when our kids, when our girls are grown,

36
00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:49.220
that there's a lot more of people that look like us,

37
00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:50.520
that are in this position

38
00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:54.230
because we're still so underrepresented

39
00:01:54.230 --> 00:01:56.698
in all of business, in government,

40
00:01:56.698 --> 00:01:58.230
and all these different ways.

41
00:01:58.230 --> 00:02:01.190
So I hope that by me being here,

42
00:02:01.190 --> 00:02:03.750
it's like a little tiny step forward, and we can chip away.

43
00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:06.460
By you being here, chip away, right?

44
00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:09.930
And so let's do our part in just making sure I think,

45
00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:12.600
and just making it easier for everyone

46
00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:15.570
to be represented in every sector.

47
00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:18.300
I'm really excited that we got to this point.

48
00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:19.696
I just can't believe!

49
00:02:19.696 --> 00:02:20.529
(Jessica laughs)
I'm so happy.

50
00:02:20.529 --> 00:02:21.810
And by the way, I am also--

51
00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:23.050
Just remember "Never Been Kissed."

52
00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:23.883
Wait, no.
(Jessica laughs)

53
00:02:23.883 --> 00:02:25.920
Okay, wait, look, I pulled up some pictures for us.

54
00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:26.753
There we go.
Yup.

55
00:02:26.753 --> 00:02:28.490
I mean, that was us
(Jessica laughs)

56
00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:29.760
back in the day.
(Jessica laughing)

57
00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:32.400
It's a true story.
I mean, (mumbles)

58
00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.640
It's wild, it's so wild.

59
00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:36.060
And you believed in me

60
00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:38.980
when I was just a scrappy little nugget

61
00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:39.813
(audience laughs)

62
00:02:39.813 --> 00:02:41.510
just trying to like, get a job.

63
00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:43.280
I have another picture pulled up

64
00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:46.430
where I'm with you at a Baby2Baby event.

65
00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:50.120
I'm pregnant with my second daughter, Frankie in that.

66
00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:55.120
And I have looked to you as an example,

67
00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:59.363
honestly, as a mom and a friend,

68
00:03:00.702 --> 00:03:02.470
what you guys do with Baby2Baby,

69
00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:04.670
and I'm just so passionate about what you do.

70
00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:07.470
You give back so much.

71
00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:10.450
You set the tone for how we're supposed to be.

72
00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:12.880
Now all of my cleaning products

73
00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:16.350
are in your sustainable bottles and--

74
00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:19.090
Actually, a lot of my sustainability

75
00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:22.740
or the values around sustainability come from my grandma,

76
00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:25.340 line:15%
just to bring it back around to why we're here today

77
00:03:27.225 --> 00:03:31.770 line:15%
and paying homage to our family and our family members.

78
00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:36.140 line:15%
But my grandma, she would re-use and recycle everything.

79
00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:38.410
Really?
Every margarine tub,

80
00:03:38.410 --> 00:03:39.820
that was our Tupperware.

81
00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:42.400
We didn't go and buy extra stuff.

82
00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:45.450
She would MacGyver everything,

83
00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:47.750
everything had five different uses.

84
00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:52.170
And so I take that sensibility when I was building Honest.

85
00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:55.320
You know, you pull a lot from your own life experience

86
00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:59.730
and then up-cycling, recycling, reusing,

87
00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:02.300
that's just embedded in who I am as a person.

88
00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:04.250
I hate wasting things, you know?

89
00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:05.550
That's exactly why you went

90
00:04:05.550 --> 00:04:06.383
on that path.
(Jessica chuckles)

91
00:04:06.383 --> 00:04:08.760
And I went on the path that my mother set as an example

92
00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:10.763
which was Studio 54 in Hollywood.

93
00:04:10.763 --> 00:04:11.596
(Jessica laughs)
(audience laughs)

94
00:04:11.596 --> 00:04:15.560
So, now I'm awake in this new arena,

95
00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:17.170
and I love our lives.

96
00:04:17.170 --> 00:04:18.640
I wouldn't trade them for a thing.

97
00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:20.711
Reading this book, I was like, I wanna quit my job.

98
00:04:20.711 --> 00:04:21.544
(Jessica laughs)

99
00:04:21.544 --> 00:04:24.120
And this is the best book I've ever read

100
00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:26.790
in the cookbook world, because it is a lifestyle.

101
00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:29.640
It is a philosophy and a mentality

102
00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:32.640
and we're gonna meet the authors of "Grand Dishes".

103
00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:35.080
Please welcome Iska Lupton,

104
00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:39.245
and Anastasia Miari.
(audience applauding)

105
00:04:39.245 --> 00:04:40.504
Hi, Drew...
(clapping continues)

106
00:04:40.504 --> 00:04:41.703
Hi, Anastasia.
(Jessica chuckles)

107
00:04:41.703 --> 00:04:44.690
How are you?
(Anastasia mumbles)

108
00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:45.670
We're good. We're good.

109
00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:47.140
Yia yia just got very excited

110
00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:49.394
'cause she saw you holding her book in your hand.

111
00:04:49.394 --> 00:04:50.500
(all laughing)

112
00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:52.980
I mean, that is your Yia yia

113
00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:54.907
and she's right here on the cover.

114
00:04:54.907 --> 00:04:55.740
Yeah.

115
00:04:55.740 --> 00:04:59.290
Anastasia, how did the idea of this book come about?

116
00:04:59.290 --> 00:05:03.830
Because it's so different than other cookbooks to me,

117
00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:06.540
because it's beautiful recipes

118
00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:09.060
but you transport us around the world,

119
00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:13.860
and you give us wisdom beyond even the dishes.

120
00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:16.450
Yeah, so it started because I was living in the UK

121
00:05:16.450 --> 00:05:18.490 line:15%
at the time, and I really just wanted a cook book

122
00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:20.410 line:15%
of my own Yia yia's recipes

123
00:05:20.410 --> 00:05:23.190 line:15%
because she cooks amazingly well.

124
00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:27.400 line:15%
And then I guess we did a shoot with Yia yia,

125
00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:29.670
and Iska and our best friend, Ella,

126
00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:31.370
and the photographs came back amazing.

127
00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:33.800
So we put them on our Instagram account,

128
00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:35.570
and then people started contacting us

129
00:05:35.570 --> 00:05:37.140
about their own grandmothers.

130
00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:40.400
So we decided to sort of go ahead and make it a global thing

131
00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:43.500
and cook with grannies from all over the world.

132
00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:47.350
Anastasia, do you have the same name as your Yia yia?

133
00:05:47.350 --> 00:05:48.183
Yes, I do.

134
00:05:48.183 --> 00:05:50.960 line:15%
So (laughs) in Greece,

135
00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:53.670 line:15%
the grandchildren always get their grandparents names.

136
00:05:53.670 --> 00:05:57.030 line:15%
So you're always an ode to your own grandparents.

137
00:05:57.030 --> 00:06:00.000 line:15%
So, Anastasia, and Anastasia.

138
00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:01.250
We both have the same name. (laughs)

139
00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:02.570
In Latino culture,

140
00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:06.260
a lot of mothers and daughters have the same name.

141
00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:07.093
Really?

142
00:06:07.093 --> 00:06:08.160
Mm-hmm. A lot of, yeah.

143
00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:09.660 line:15%
And then there are certain cultures

144
00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:12.880 line:15%
that men and their sons have the same name

145
00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:13.770 line:15%
which could be a lot--

146
00:06:13.770 --> 00:06:15.110 line:15%
It's more common place.
to live up to.

147
00:06:15.110 --> 00:06:15.943
Yeah.
(both chuckling)

148
00:06:15.943 --> 00:06:17.440
Yes. Exactly.

149
00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:21.074
I love my grandmother's name. It's Deloris.

150
00:06:21.074 --> 00:06:21.940
Wow. Yeah.
I thought about it.

151
00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:24.280
I voted for it for my daughter, Frankie

152
00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:26.180
'cause I was like, in short it's Dolly.

153
00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:29.150
It's so cute, but she was a Frankie.

154
00:06:29.150 --> 00:06:31.620
But I love the meaning behind this book.

155
00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:33.560
And we're gonna take a quick commercial break,

156
00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:35.220
and then we're going to come back,

157
00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:37.710
and Iska, what are we gonna cook after the break?

158
00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:40.610
We are making Yia yia's roasted vegetables,

159
00:06:40.610 --> 00:06:42.718 line:15%
which are actually on the cover of the book,

160
00:06:42.718 --> 00:06:43.551 line:15%
(Drew gasps)

161
00:06:43.551 --> 00:06:45.540
and then Tzatziki to go with it,

162
00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:47.832
which is a kind of garlicky yogurt dill.

163
00:06:47.832 --> 00:06:49.760
I love to Tzatziki.

164
00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:52.927
(bright upbeat music)

